WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.666
YUFENG GUO: In this
thrilling conclusion

00:00:01.666 --> 00:00:04.320
to our video on training
big models in the cloud,

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.274
I'll help you scale your compute
power from machine learning.

00:00:07.274 --> 00:00:08.940
Will our training
have enough resources?

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.530
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.180
In our previous
episode, we talked

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.580
about the problems that are
encountered when your data is

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.890
too big to fit on
your local machine,

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.830
and we discussed how we can move
that data off onto the cloud

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.510
to have scalable storage.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.090
Today, we move on to the
second half of that problem--

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.760
getting those compute
resources wrangled together.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.010
When training larger
models, the current approach

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.770
involves doing
training in parallel.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.410
What this means is that our
data gets split up and sent

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.720
to many worker machines,
And then the model must

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.280
put the information
in signals it's

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.180
getting back together to
create the fully trained model.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.040
Now, you could spin up
some virtual machines

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.900
and install the
network libraries,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.610
network them together,
configure them

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.440
to run distributed
machine learning,

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.940
and then when you
finish, you'd want

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.650
to take down those machines.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.132
While this may
seem easy to some,

00:00:54.132 --> 00:00:55.590
it can be a challenge
if you're not

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.440
familiar with things like
installing GBU drivers

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.810
and debugging
compatibility problems

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.690
between different versions
of the underlying libraries.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.600
So today we'll use Cloud Machine
Learning Engine's training

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.970
functionality to
go from Python code

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.650
to train model with no
infrastructure work needed.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.730
The service automatically
acquires and configures

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.860
resources as needed
and shuts them down

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.850
when it's done training.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.280
There are three main steps to
using Cloud Machine Learning

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.000
Engine--

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.952
packaging your Python code,
creating a configuration file

00:01:24.952 --> 00:01:26.410
that describes the
kind of machines

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.530
you want, and submitting your
training job to the cloud.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:31.030
Let's see how to
set up our training

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.750
to take advantage
of this service.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.060
We've moved our Python code
from our Jupyter notebook

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.100
out into a separate
script on its own.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.470
Let's call that file task.py.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.470
This is going to act as our
Python module, which will

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.330
be called from other files.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:49.170
Now, we want to wrap task.py
inside a Python package.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.170
Python packages are made
by placing the module

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.580
inside another folder--

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:54.810
let's call it trainer--

00:01:54.810 --> 00:02:01.645
and placing an empty file,
__init__.py, alongside test.py.

00:02:01.645 --> 00:02:04.020
So our final file structure
is made up of a folder called

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.009
trainer containing two files,
the __init__py and the task.py

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:09.090
files.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.130
While our package
is called trainer

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.130
our module path is trainer.task.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.012
If you wanted to break out
the code into more components,

00:02:17.012 --> 00:02:18.970
you would include those
in this folder as well.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.470
For example, you might
have, say, a util.py

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:23.370
in the trainer folder.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.220
Once our code is
packaged up, it's time

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.980
to create a configuration
file to specify what machines

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.420
you want running your training.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:30.878
You can choose to
run your training

00:02:30.878 --> 00:02:33.290
with just a small number
of machines, as few as one

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.390
or, many machines with
GPUs attached to them.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.750
There are a few predefined
specifications, which

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.290
make it easy to get started.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:41.840
And once you grow
out of those, you

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.340
can configure a
custom architecture

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:45.310
to your heart's content.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:46.980
We've got our Python
code packaged up,

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.750
and we have our configuration
file written out.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.260
So let's move on to the step
you've all been waiting for,

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.430
the training.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.980
To submit a training job, we'll
use the gcloud command line

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.760
tool and run gcloud ml-engine
jobs submit training.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.520
There is also an
equivalent REST API call.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.490
We specify a unique job name,
the package path and module

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.980
name, the region for
your job to run in,

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.780
and a cloud storage
directory to place

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.250
the outputs of your training.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.830
Be sure to use the same region
as where your data is stored

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.750
to get optimal performance.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.700
Once you run this command,
your Python package

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.510
is going to get zipped up and
uploaded to the directory we

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:21.480
just specified.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.130
From there, the
package will be run

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:24.990
in the cloud on the
machines that we

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.460
specified in the configuration.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.910
You can monitor your training
job in the Cloud Console

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.430
by going to ML Engine
and selecting Jobs.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.470
There, we will see a list of
all the jobs we've ever run,

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:37.030
including the current job.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:40.230
You can also see a timer on
how much time the job has taken

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.420
so far and a link
to the logs that

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.710
are coming out of the model.

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:47.850
Our code exports the training
model to our cloud storage path

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.830
that we have provided
in the job directory.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.290
So from here, we can easily
point to prediction service

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.900
directly at the outputs and
create a prediction service,

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.780
as we learned about Episode
4, Serverless Predictions

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.070
at Scale.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.630
Using Cloud Machine
Learning Engine,

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:02.370
we can achieve
distributed training

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.770
without dealing with
infrastructure ourselves.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.710
As a result, we can spend
more time with our data.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:10.890
Simply package up the code,
add that configuration file,

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.661
and submit the training job.

00:04:12.661 --> 00:04:14.160
If you want the
nitty gritty details

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.470
on TensorFlow's
distributed training model

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.470
check out this in-depth talk
from the TensorFlow Dev Center.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.170
But for now, remember,
spend less time building

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.760
distributed systems and
more time with your data

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.776
by training your model using
Cloud Machine Learning Engine.

00:04:28.776 --> 00:04:32.130
I'm Yufeng Guo, and thanks for
watching this episode of Cloud

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:33.390
AI Adventures.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.950
If you enjoyed it, please go
ahead and hit that like button.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:37.560
And for more machine
learning action,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.060
be sure to subscribe
to the channel

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.280
to catch future episodes
